1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:01,651
Hey, everyone,

2
00:00:01,651 --> 00:00:03,799
let's start with a simple question.

3
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:06,360
Why do things get cheaper the more you do them?

4
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:09,738
Why does your first try at building something feel slow ...

5
00:00:09,738 --> 00:00:12,297
and expensive, but by the fifth or tenth time,

6
00:00:12,297 --> 00:00:13,509
it's easier, faster,

7
00:00:13,509 --> 00:00:14,519
and cheaper to do?

8
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:16,960
That's not just your brain getting better.

9
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:20,280
It's a real business principle called the learning curve.

10
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:24,040
And the learning rate is what helps us measure that improvement.

11
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:26,873
It's going to show us how much more efficient you ...

12
00:00:26,873 --> 00:00:28,839
become as total output increases.

13
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:31,161
So today, what we're going to look at is ...

14
00:00:31,161 --> 00:00:34,011
what the learning curve is, how it's different from ...

15
00:00:34,011 --> 00:00:36,331
economies of scale, and how to calculate a ...

16
00:00:36,331 --> 00:00:38,518
learning rate using some real numbers.

17
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:40,000
Let's jump into it.

18
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,754
The learning curve shows how average cost per unit ...

19
00:00:43,754 --> 00:00:47,959
decreases as cumulative total output or cumulative output rises.

20
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:50,664
This happens because people get faster,

21
00:00:50,664 --> 00:00:53,358
they make less mistakes, tools get better,

22
00:00:53,358 --> 00:00:55,079
and the processes improve.

23
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:00,400
In the production process, we usually express this as a learning rate.

24
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:01,998
For example, an 80%

25
00:01:01,998 --> 00:01:06,269
learning rate means that every time total production ...

26
00:01:06,269 --> 00:01:09,021
doubles, the average cost per unit ...

27
00:01:09,021 --> 00:01:11,678
drops to 80% of what it was before.

28
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,560
Let's look at how this looks graphically.

29
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:17,497
Here we have a cumulative total output,

30
00:01:17,497 --> 00:01:20,399
or excuse me, an average cost curve here.

31
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:22,611
Cost per unit on our vertical axis,

32
00:01:22,611 --> 00:01:24,959
cumulative output on the horizontal axis.

33
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:27,706
Now, let's have the learning effect ...

34
00:01:27,706 --> 00:01:30,119
take place in our learning curve,

35
00:01:30,119 --> 00:01:33,998
and we're going to shift down this average cost curve.

36
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:37,153
And the reason this takes place is because of the ...

37
00:01:37,153 --> 00:01:39,959
learning rate, the learning effect as well.

38
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:43,295
So notice on the top average cost curve,

39
00:01:43,295 --> 00:01:46,120
I was producing one unit of output,

40
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:49,238
and it cost me $10,000 per unit to do so.

41
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:52,210
But because of the learning effect,

42
00:01:52,210 --> 00:01:54,680
learning rates, what happens?

43
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:58,760
We shift that, that average cost curve down.

44
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:01,089
And now if we were to go back,

45
00:02:01,089 --> 00:02:03,959
we can make that first unit at $8,000.

46
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:06,186
But in reality, as we're doubling our ...

47
00:02:06,186 --> 00:02:08,719
production here, we're going to be producing ...

48
00:02:08,719 --> 00:02:11,852
that second unit for a little bit cheaper than what we're ...

49
00:02:11,852 --> 00:02:15,118
actually producing or what we would have produced before.

50
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:18,022
So you'll notice that at the second unit,

51
00:02:18,022 --> 00:02:20,595
if we were on the original average cost curve,

52
00:02:20,595 --> 00:02:23,439
it it would have cost us slightly less than 10,000.

53
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:25,784
But because of learning effect,

54
00:02:25,784 --> 00:02:27,879
we've learned how to do stuff better.

55
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:30,554
We have an improvement in technology and improvement ...

56
00:02:30,554 --> 00:02:31,879
in tools, all those things.

57
00:02:32,040 --> 00:02:34,425
What takes place is we can actually now produce that ...

58
00:02:34,425 --> 00:02:35,479
second unit of output.

59
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:38,091
We have doubled our output in this case,

60
00:02:38,091 --> 00:02:41,208
but we can produce that second unit of output at a ...

61
00:02:41,208 --> 00:02:43,078
lower average cost per unit.

62
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:45,662
Now, let's make sure that we are ...

63
00:02:45,662 --> 00:02:48,272
not confusing the learning effect,

64
00:02:48,272 --> 00:02:50,708
which would be the Distance here,

65
00:02:50,708 --> 00:02:53,840
the learning rate with economies of scale.

66
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:57,826
Economies of scale are going to be spreading the fixed cost ...

67
00:02:57,826 --> 00:02:59,839
over a large amount of output.

68
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:03,233
And so as we move down one average cost curve,

69
00:03:03,233 --> 00:03:07,039
we're going to have economies of scale, all right?

70
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:11,320
That's a lowering of your average total cost over output.

71
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:15,364
The learning effects that comes from getting better at ...

72
00:03:15,364 --> 00:03:17,453
work, having improvements in ...

73
00:03:17,453 --> 00:03:20,044
technology, making fewer mistakes,

74
00:03:20,044 --> 00:03:23,554
having faster labor, all those things that go into ...

75
00:03:23,554 --> 00:03:28,318
shifting down this average cost curve to a lower cost per unit basis.

76
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:33,360
Now, let's look at how this works in an example.

77
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:34,977
So, we have a company building ...

78
00:03:34,977 --> 00:03:36,239
a new electric scooter.

79
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:38,560
The first unit costs 10,000 build.

80
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:43,620
After building two scooters, the average cost per scooter ...

81
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:45,520
has dropped to $8,000.

82
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:49,122
So similar to what we were looking at in a previous ...

83
00:03:49,122 --> 00:03:49,981
graph, now,

84
00:03:49,981 --> 00:03:52,386
scooter one was $10,000 to build,

85
00:03:52,386 --> 00:03:54,877
but scooter two is $6,000 to build.

86
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,920
Total cost $16,000 to build those two scooters.

87
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:02,914
The average cost to build scooter one was $10,001 ...

88
00:04:02,914 --> 00:04:05,999
divided by the lone scooter, or 10,000.

89
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:10,092
And the average cost to build the two scooters is 16,000 ...

90
00:04:10,092 --> 00:04:13,138
divided by two, which is going to give us the ...

91
00:04:13,138 --> 00:04:14,999
8,000 that we saw earlier.

92
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:17,720
Now, let's calculate the learning rate.

93
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:19,760
So we're going to bring in this formula here.

94
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:22,651
The learning rate is equal to the average cost of the ...

95
00:04:22,651 --> 00:04:25,809
second scooter or the fourth scooter or the eighth scooter ...

96
00:04:25,809 --> 00:04:28,967
or whatever it happens to be, divided by the average cost of ...

97
00:04:28,967 --> 00:04:29,998
the first scooter.

98
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:32,038
We're going to multiply that by 100%,

99
00:04:32,038 --> 00:04:34,439
and that's going to give us our learning rate.

100
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:36,285
So, so here we have $8,000,

101
00:04:36,285 --> 00:04:40,519
the average cost of building the two scooters divided by 10,000.

102
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:43,364
And we're going to multiply that by 100%,

103
00:04:43,364 --> 00:04:44,679
and that gives us 80%.

104
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:49,660
So what that means is every time output doubles,

105
00:04:49,660 --> 00:04:54,040
average cost drops to 80% of the previous level.

106
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:56,300
If we were to lock in on that 80%

107
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:58,820
and we get no more learning effects,

108
00:04:58,820 --> 00:05:00,920
then, then what we could see is ...

109
00:05:00,920 --> 00:05:05,877
output continues to double as we go from two scooters to four scooters.

110
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,201
So the average cost of doing four scooters,

111
00:05:09,201 --> 00:05:11,957
we would do 80% times the 8,000 what we ...

112
00:05:11,957 --> 00:05:15,158
previously done, and that gives us $6,400.

113
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:18,442
Now go from four scooters to eight scooters,

114
00:05:18,442 --> 00:05:20,664
and we're maintaining that 80%

115
00:05:20,664 --> 00:05:23,655
learning rate, and that gives us a total of ...

116
00:05:23,655 --> 00:05:27,758
5,120 as the average cost of making those eight scooters.

117
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:30,960
We're going to continue to do this with 16 scooters.

118
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:34,451
80% our learning rate times the ...

119
00:05:34,451 --> 00:05:39,319
previous $5,120 and now that gives us $4,096.

120
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:42,554
So what we can see here is that the learning effect is ...

121
00:05:42,554 --> 00:05:45,914
going to allow us to continue to lower the average cost of ...

122
00:05:45,914 --> 00:05:47,558
producing those scooters.

123
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:49,495
And again, this is different from ...

124
00:05:49,495 --> 00:05:52,068
economies of scale, which maintains us on one ...

125
00:05:52,068 --> 00:05:54,708
average cost curve, whereas the learning rate ...

126
00:05:54,708 --> 00:05:57,958
allows us to shift different down our average cost curve.

127
00:05:58,120 --> 00:06:00,560
So strategically, why this matters?

128
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:02,880
Well, it helps businesses do a lot of things.

129
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:06,710
It helps them forecast how fast their costs will fall,

130
00:06:06,710 --> 00:06:10,416
it helps them decide when to scale up and it also helps ...

131
00:06:10,416 --> 00:06:14,038
them price products more competitively over time.

132
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:16,348
So these big companies like Tesla,

133
00:06:16,348 --> 00:06:19,089
Boeing and Apple, who are huge in production ...

134
00:06:19,089 --> 00:06:21,237
costs, they can use these learning ...

135
00:06:21,237 --> 00:06:24,718
curves to plan staffing, pricing and production goals.

136
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:27,480
And startups in particular can use them,

137
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:31,440
the learning rates, that is to help survive early. High cost.

